/*
 Deliberately empty, and it is the *directory* that does the work rather than this file.

 This app has no localisation to switch: every string in it is a Russian literal, and
 `CLAUDE.md` records that as a decision rather than an omission. What it did have was a bundle
 that claimed no localisations at all, which is a different thing and had two visible costs.

 macOS renders the standard menus — the application menu, «Правка», «Окно», «Справка» and the
 Services submenu — in the app's own effective localisation, not in the user's language. With
 no `.lproj` and no `CFBundleDevelopmentRegion`, that resolved to English, so a program whose
 every word is Russian carried an English menu bar even on a Russian system. Measured on the
 same binary under three configurations: with `-AppleLanguages '(ru)'` and no localisation the
 menu stayed «Edit / Copy / Paste / Quit»; with `CFBundleDevelopmentRegion = ru` and this
 directory present it became «Правка / Скопировать / Вставить / Завершить».

 The second cost is quieter. `Bundle.main.preferredLocalizations` answered `["en"]`, which is
 what forced `RussianCopy.modelSize` and the temperature slider in `SettingsModelsView` to pin
 `Locale(identifier: "ru_RU")` by hand so that a Russian sentence would not sit next to «4.8».
 Those pins stay — they are correct regardless of the machine's language, which is the point
 of pinning them — but the reason they were unavoidable is gone.

 Why the file is empty and must stay that way: a `Text("Строка")` in SwiftUI goes through
 `LocalizedStringKey`, so declaring a localisation makes every one of them a lookup into this
 table. Measured, with this file empty: `NSLocalizedString` and `String(localized:)` return the
 key unchanged, including a key containing a `%`. Adding an entry here would start overriding
 the literals in the source, which is exactly the drift `CONTEXT.md` exists to prevent — the
 same sentence in two places, disagreeing.

 `Scripts/make-app-bundle.sh` copies this directory into `Contents/Resources` before `codesign`
 runs, for the same reason it does that with the icon: the signature covers `Resources`, and a
 resource added afterwards breaks the seal — which costs the Accessibility grant that the
 script's signing identity exists to preserve.
*/
